oil and boil for 10 hours under reflux conditions. \nThen add 150 g of  lithium sulphate (Li2SO4 · H2O) and a few drops of \nbromine and boil once more for 15 minutes. Allow to cool and make \nup to one liter with distilled water.  \n3.3. Anhydrous sodium carbonate, Na2CO3, made up into a 20 % w/v \nsolution. \nOIV-MA-F1-13 : 2015                                                                       2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAgric. Biol. Chem., 49 (11), 3331–3332, 1985 (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/bbb1961/49/11/49_11_3331/_pdf)\nciteturn41search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.7 years ago; Crawled: 19.0 years ago; The S medium used for the test contained (per liter of distilled water): sodium succinate, 1.0 g; KH₂PO₄, 0.5 g; K₂HPO₄, 0.6 g; (NH₄)₂SO₄, 1.0 g; MgSO₄·7H₂O, 0.2 g; NaCl, 0.2 g; trace element solution,⁶) 1.0 ml; vitamin solution,⁶) 1.0 ml; gelatin, 7.0 g; and Agar Noble (Difco), 12 g.\n⁵)\n\nThe present paper reports that *R. capsulata* is able to hydrolyze gelatin in the presence of calcium ions, differing from *R. gelatinosa*, which does not require the ion for the hydrolysis.\n\nThree strains of *R. gelatinosa* and 10 strains of *R. capsulata* were used for the test. *R. gelatinosa* ATCC 17011 and A 1 were supplied by Dr. Y. Nishimura (Science University of Tokyo, Noda) and Dr. H. Takahashi (Tohoku University, Sendai), respectively, and *R. capsulata* ATCC 11166 by Dr. A. Yoshikawa (Mitsubishi Gas Chemical Co., Niigata). Other strains were our isolates from aquatic environments.\n\nThe S medium used for the test contained (per liter of distilled water): sodium succinate, 1.0 g; KH₂PO₄, 0.5 g; K₂HPO₄, 0.6 g; (NH₄)₂SO₄, 1.0 g; MgSO₄·7H₂O, 0.2 g; NaCl, 0.2 g; trace element solution,⁶) 1.0 ml; vitamin solution,⁶) 1.0 ml; gelatin, 7.0 g; and Agar Noble (Difco), 12 g. The medium, with a pH of 6.7, was autoclaved for 15 min at 120°C. Where indicated, a 0.2% CaCl₂ solution sterilized by autoclaving was added to the melted S medium just before pouring into plates, to give a final concentration of 0.02%.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTunable multi-electron redox polyoxometalates for decoupled water splitting driven by sunlight (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-58622-8.pdf)\nciteturn41search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: 1.6 years ago; Sodium tungstate dihydrate (Na₂WO₄·2H₂O, AR > 99.5%), Hydrochloric acid solution (HCl, AR, 36.0–38.0%), Phosphoric acid (H₃PO₄, AR > 85.0%), Potassium chloride (KCl, AR ≥ 99.5%), hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂, AR, 30%), sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄, AR, 95.0–98.0%), nitric acid (HNO₃, AR, 65.0–68.0%), Potassium bicarbonate (KHCO₃, ACS, 99.7–100.5%), Sodium perchlorate monohydrate (NaClO₄·H₂O, AR ≥ 99.0%), Sodium chloride (NaCl, AR, ≥ 99.5%), Sodium hydroxide (NaOH, AR ≥ 96%), Sodium sulfate (Na₂SO₄, AR ≥ 99.0%), Bismuth(III) nitrate pentahydrate (Bi(NO₃)₃·5H₂O, AR ≥ 99.0%), Ammonium hydroxide aqueous solution (NH₃·H₂O, 25.0–28.0%) and Sodium carbonate (Na₂CO₃, AR ≥ 99.8%) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd.\n## Methods\n\n### Materials\n\nSodium tungstate dihydrate (Na₂WO₄·2H₂O, AR > 99.5%), Hydrochloric acid solution (HCl, AR, 36.0–38.0%), Phosphoric acid (H₃PO₄, AR > 85.0%), Potassium chloride (KCl, AR ≥ 99.5%), hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂, AR, 30%), sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄, AR, 95.0–98.0%), nitric acid (HNO₃, AR, 65.0–68.0%), Potassium bicarbonate (KHCO₃, ACS, 99.7–100.5%), Sodium perchlorate monohydrate (NaClO₄·H₂O, AR ≥ 99.0%), Sodium chloride (NaCl, AR, ≥ 99.5%), Sodium hydroxide (NaOH, AR ≥ 96%), Sodium sulfate (Na₂SO₄, AR ≥ 99.0%), Bismuth(III) nitrate pentahydrate (Bi(NO₃)₃·5H₂O, AR ≥ 99.0%), Ammonium hydroxide aqueous solution (NH₃·H₂O, 25.0–28.0%) and Sodium carbonate (Na₂CO₃, AR ≥ 99.8%) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Vanadium(V) trichloride oxide (VOCl₃, 95%), Ammonium metavanadate (NH₄VO₃, AR, 99%) and Deuterium oxide (D₂O, 99.9 atom % D) were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCrystal growth, structure and physical  properties of crystals (https://library.iem.ac.ru/exper/v8_2/six.pdf)\nciteturn41search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 25.7 years ago; Hydrothermal synthesis and properties of sodium pentatungstate monohydrate Na₂W₅O₁₆·H₂O** ... % WO₃, showing fields labeled MELT, Na₂WO₄ on the left and WO₃ on the right.\nCrystal growth, structure and physical properties of crystals\n\nSpectroscopy (Physical Electronics Industries, Edina, Minnesota, 1972).\n\n**Redkin A.F., Ponomarev V.I. and Kostromin N.P. Hydrothermal synthesis and properties of sodium pentatungstate monohydrate Na₂W₅O₁₆·H₂O**\n\nkey words: *[hydrothermal synthesis, sodium polytungstates, unit-cell parameters, phase diagram]*\n\nNa₂W₅O₁₆·H₂O was first obtained by hydrothermal synthesis from WO₃ in solution of 5-15 wt.% NaCl at 500°C, 400-500 bars as colorless mica-like crystals with specific weight 7.007±0.005 g/cm³. It loses water in the range 440-660°C and transforms into Na₂W₅O₁₆ which incongruently melts at 770±5°C.\n\nIt was identified that Na₂W₅O₁₆·H₂O has a triclinic unit-cell with parameters: a=17.91(1) Å, b=26.92(1) Å, c=39.93(1) Å, α=99.5(1)°, β=91.9(1)°, γ=98.6(1)°, V=18734 Å³, and z=64. The X-ray pattern of the compound is presented in Table 1.\n\nPhase diagram of sodium polytungstate stability in the right part of Na₂O-WO₃ system was refined. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nic0c00594 1..9 (https://pub.epsilon.slu.se/17455/1/smiechowski_m_persson_i_200826.pdf)\nciteturn41search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Sodium permanganate monohydrate, NaMnO4·H2O ... tungstate dihydrate, Na2WO4·2H2O (Merck, p.a.), and sodium\n2.83\n123\n12\n24\n■MATERIALS AND METHODS\nChemicals. Sodium permanganate monohydrate, NaMnO4·H2O\n(A